    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "656f9df0_f7bd8b56",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/pv_iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 35
      },
      "lineNbr": 339,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-11-24T11:35:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This makes it insecure, right? We should never enable Device TLB (i.e. effectively let the device bypass the IOMMU) unless we know that the device is trusted to implement Device TLB securely independently of the host, and we use is_dev_in_satc() as the criterion for that.\n\nTherefore, we have 2 cases:\n- The device is not in SATC, but the host asks pKVM to enable device TLB for it, by passing ats_supported\u003d1. In such can we should _deny_ that to the host.\n- The device is in SATC. In such case, according to the comment I pasted above, in legacy mode Device TLB is enabled transparently by hardware anyway, and setting CONTEXT_TT_DEV_IOTLB is redundant and undesirable (and the host doesn\u0027t do it).\n\nTherefore, we should just always set CONTEXT_TT_MULTI_LEVEL, and we don\u0027t need the ats_supported parameter for this hypercall (for legacy mode), right?\n\nTherefore,",
      "parentUuid": "f6d07292_e2e742a5",
      "revId": "c7ec47177158bdc7e2f56e23dc9f5ca6c8b2d929",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1a6ee089_5cc8c187",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/pv_iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 35
      },
      "lineNbr": 339,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-11-24T12:27:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e The device is not in SATC, but the host asks pKVM to enable device TLB for it, by passing ats_supported\u003d1. In such can we should deny that to the host.\n\nYou are right, we could use `is_dev_in_satc` to validate that.\n\n\u003e The device is in SATC. In such case, according to the comment I pasted above, in legacy mode Device TLB is enabled transparently by hardware anyway, and setting CONTEXT_TT_DEV_IOTLB is redundant and undesirable (and the host doesn\u0027t do it).\n\nThe host actually does it I think:\nhttps://android.googlesource.com/kernel/common/+/refs/heads/android16-6.12-desktop/drivers/iommu/intel/iommu.c#2735\n\nThats the reason I thought I shall keep this change.\n\nFrom reading the code and VT-d spec(8.8), what I understand is - ATC_REQUIRED flag is set in SATC if all the devices enumerated in the table need ATC to be enabled. I think this would make the hardware enable it transparently. if ATC_REQUIRED is unset, then it might not be transparent and host driver sets tt\u003dCONTEXT_TT_DEV_IOTLB. VT-d spec also says, the device should be able to function(with probably reduced performance) if ATC is not enabled. So it would not harm not to set CONTEXT_TT_DEV_IOTLB I guess.\n\nWhat do you feel about adding `is_dev_in_satc()` check for security validation and keeping this change? I shall remove this code if you feel its not worth doing it for legacy mode",
      "parentUuid": "656f9df0_f7bd8b56",
      "revId": "c7ec47177158bdc7e2f56e23dc9f5ca6c8b2d929",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6d6affe9_6ce25633",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/pv_iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 35
      },
      "lineNbr": 339,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-11-24T13:07:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ok, after a closer look, I see dmar_ats_supported() actually returns true in legacy mode in the case when ATS is supported but _not_ required by the given device (i.e. if the device is in SATC but with ATC_REQUIRED\u003d0). So yeah, to support this, we can let the host pass ats_supported\u003d1 (although we have no hardware to test that on). But yeah, we absolutely should check is_dev_in_satc() here before allowing that.\n\n(According to VT-d spec section 4.4, any devices in SATC are required to implement \"HPA isolation boundary\" and thus are trusted, regardless of their ATC_REQUIRED value.)\n\n\u003e ATC_REQUIRED flag is set in SATC if all the devices enumerated in the table need ATC to be enabled. I think this would make the hardware enable it transparently.\n\nI believe in scalable mode it does _not_ make the hardware enable it transparently. We saw that on PTL: devices with ATC_REQUIRED didn\u0027t work successfully when the Device TLB Enable bit was cleared in their scalable-mode context entries. But in legacy mode, as this comment in the IOMMU driver code suggests, it is different (I\u0027m not sure if this difference is documented in the VT-d spec).",
      "parentUuid": "1a6ee089_5cc8c187",
      "revId": "c7ec47177158bdc7e2f56e23dc9f5ca6c8b2d929",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
